  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nFor today and into tomorrow our weather remains mostly dictated\nby an upper level ridge over the plains and surface high\npressure over the Appalachians. Tomrorow's conditions look\nsimilar to today with temperatures perhaps slightly warmer and \na moderate east to southeast wind as the surface pressure \ngradient slowly starts to react to the presence of Hurricane \nIsaias. Relative humidity values are forecast to fall to 20 to \n30 percent in the afternoon with east winds at about 5 to 8 mph.\nThese are elevated fire danger conditions from a purely \nmeteorological standpoint although fuels are not quite yet fully\nreceptive. Highs on Friday look to approach daily records.\n\nHurricane Isaias is on-track this afternoon. Hurricane Hunter\naircraft show a steady increase in intensity, in line with\nprevious forecasts and the storm is projected to cross the AL/FL\ncoastline Friday night. A shortwave trough presently over the\nTexas Big Bend interacts with the cyclone as it moves onshore\nhelping it accelerate and max a quick extra-tropical transition.\nFavorable jet-level lift ahead of the system should lead to\nrainfall starting Saturday morning across most of the area with\nthe center of the cyclone pulling northwestward into west \nTennessee by midday. The peak of any stronger winds should be \nSaturday afternoon mainly across western Kentucky with gusts \n25-30 mph currently forecast. Storm total rain of 1 to 1.5 \ninches across western Kentucky decreasing to 0.5 to 0.75 inches \nfurther north and west remains on track. This should not be \nenough to cause flooding issues given the antecedent conditions.\nHodographs/shear are favorable for tornadoes but for now \ninstability appears very limited with surface temperatures in \nthe lower 60s and dewpoints in the upper 50s.\n\nThe more benign passage that only results in impacts to planned\noutdoor events with a steady most of the day rain is about the \n80-85% confidence scenario. The contingency would be if the \nstorm were to rapidly intensify well-beyond the current forecast\nintensity prior to landfall, which can happen with these semi-\nsheared storms that end up utilizing the favorable outflow \nchannel rather than be sheared apart by the southwesterly winds \naloft north of the storm. This seems less likely as time \nprogresses but we will remain alert to the possibility. If the \nstorm were to end up being stronger at landfall then the risk of\nflooding rains, stronger winds, and at least some tornado \nthreat would be higher.\n\nRidging builds back in behind the departing tropical remnants\nwith dry and relatively warm conditions forecast through at\nleast Tuesday. Wednesday-Thursday a trough begins to approach\nfrom the west and may lead to increased shower and storm \nchances but the upper ridge that establishes over Georgia looks \nlike it could be strong enough to hold everything back through \nthe end of the work week.",
